        "The reporting trap runs the other way: results so polished they hide their fragility. A single significant test among twenty run, quoted without the other nineteen; a two-decimal forecast from a model with a wide interval; a chart whose axis starts where the story needs it. Selective honesty is still dishonesty, and {S} reports that hide the full picture eventually mislead their own authors.",
        "Tool traps bite hardest under deadline: overwriting a cell that a formula referenced, sorting one column of a table without the rest, pasting values over formulas and losing the method. Each is silent, none announces itself, and all are caught by the same habits: raw data untouched, calculations scripted or locked, and a fresh-eye recomputation of at least one headline number before release.",
